      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="introduction">
        <tei:p>Recited in many Sefaradi and other non-Ashkenazi rites for the seventeenth of Tammuz, this piyyuṭ, like many 17 Tammuz seliḥot, has a stanza for each of the five tragedies discussed in the mishnah. It is here included alongside an acrostic translation.</tei:p>
        <tei:p>Note: “<j:divineName>The CAUSE</j:divineName>” is used to translate the Divine Name <j:divineName>YHVH</j:divineName>, based on the philosophical idea of <j:divineName>God</j:divineName> as the <j:divineName>Prime Mover</j:divineName> and on the interpretation of <j:divineName>the Name</j:divineName> as a causative form of the copula – “causes to be.” This translation also uses the plural pronouns <j:divineName>They</j:divineName>/<j:divineName>Their</j:divineName>/<j:divineName>Them</j:divineName> to refer to <j:divineName>God</j:divineName> as a <tei:hi rend="italic">pluralis majestatis</tei:hi>, and to avoid the implications of <j:divineName>God</j:divineName> being assigned a gender. (Find Ibn Ezra’s commentary on Genesis 1:1 for a discussion of the <tei:hi rend="italic">pluralis majestatis</tei:hi> when referring to <j:divineName>God</j:divineName>.) All divine referents (pronouns, epithets, names) are rendered in unicase.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
      <j:endConditional target="#osp_68103_introduction"/>
